    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d965">

                <pb facs="0982" n="970" xml:id="pg970"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/9393: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Yugoslavia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Belgrade</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-03-27T00:00:00+01:00">March 27,
                            1941—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received March 28—12:32 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>252. I called officially on Ninčić, newly appointed Minister for Foreign Affairs,
                    this evening. Due to his evident preoccupation, I saw him for only a few
                    moments. While, because of disturbed conditions, I had no authorization from the
                    Department to take any action constituting recognition of the Simović government
                    I considered that prompt action on my part would be important in showing
                    approval of a policy which evidently has the support of the people of this
                    country and which seems to be in accord with the views publicly expressed by the
                    President.</p>
                <p>Please telegraph urgently whether Department approves my action. I have requested
                    audiences with King Peter and General Simović.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lane</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
